1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,000
 Okay, let's continue with the breakeven analysis with more than one alternative

2
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:10,000
 starting with two alternatives that are mutually exclusive

3
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:16,000
 typical example found in almost all books on engineering economy, business economics

4
00:00:16,000 --> 00:00:21,000
 is a two alternative breakeven analysis and typically is a make or buy analysis

5
00:00:21,000 --> 00:00:27,000
 so we have the choice between making something or buying something

6
00:00:27,000 --> 00:00:32,000
 if we buy something we will only have a variable cost, if we make something we will have a fixed cost

7
00:00:32,000 --> 00:00:37,000
 and an unvariable cost which obviously will be lower than if we buy it

8
00:00:37,000 --> 00:00:40,000
 otherwise it would be a very easy decision

9
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:47,000
 here we have a breakeven value, we see that if we are having an amount that is higher than this breakeven point

10
00:00:47,000 --> 00:00:53,000
 it's more interesting to make everything that is below that breakeven point

11
00:00:53,000 --> 00:00:56,000
 it's more interesting to buy

12
00:00:56,000 --> 00:01:03,000
 okay, maybe a more realistic example

13
00:01:03,000 --> 00:01:09,000
 so we are talking about GARGEN, a company that is making home health care appliances

14
00:01:09,000 --> 00:01:15,000
 and it's a make or buy decision about a wheelchair lift to be used in a car

15
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:19,000
 well actually it's about one of the parts of that lift

16
00:01:19,000 --> 00:01:23,000
 so they can buy that arm, that steel arm

17
00:01:23,000 --> 00:01:28,000
 so that means they outsource their production and then the cost is 0.6 per unit

18
00:01:28,000 --> 00:01:32,000
 or they can decide to manufacture it in house

19
00:01:32,000 --> 00:01:37,000
 then they need two machines, machine A with a cost of 18,000

20
00:01:37,000 --> 00:01:44,000
 a life of 4 years and a 2,000 salvage value which will need an overall after 3 years

21
00:01:44,000 --> 00:01:47,000
 and that will cost 3,000 dollars

22
00:01:47,000 --> 00:01:51,000
 it's an annual operating cost is 6,000 dollars per year

23
00:01:51,000 --> 00:01:59,000
 machine B has a 12,000 dollar cost, has a life of 4 years and a minus 500 salvage value

24
00:01:59,000 --> 00:02:04,000
 and it's operating cost is 5,000 dollars per year

25
00:02:04,000 --> 00:02:07,000
 now have a look at that salvage value, quite interesting

26
00:02:07,000 --> 00:02:11,000
 here we have a negative salvage value which means that we don't receive money

27
00:02:11,000 --> 00:02:17,000
 but we will have to pay money, they call it carry away cost

28
00:02:17,000 --> 00:02:22,000
 so that means that actually we have to pay a company that is coming to pick up our machine

29
00:02:22,000 --> 00:02:25,000
 and we have to pay them for that, we do not get any money

30
00:02:25,000 --> 00:02:34,000
 also we need operators, we need 4 operators, their cost is 12,5 per hour per operator

31
00:02:34,000 --> 00:02:40,000
 and in a normal shift of 8 hours they can make a 1,000 units

32
00:02:40,000 --> 00:02:42,000
 so he has to do two things

33
00:02:43,000 --> 00:02:48,000
 to compute the number of units to be manufactured each year to justify the make option

34
00:02:48,000 --> 00:02:54,000
 and if the company expects to produce 125,000 units per year

35
00:02:54,000 --> 00:03:02,000
 and every estimate remains the same, then what is then the maximum capital expense justifiable

36
00:03:02,000 --> 00:03:08,000
 so option 1 is outsourced and this is a very simple

37
00:03:08,000 --> 00:03:16,000
 it's too very simple, part of the equation that's minus 0.6 times x

38
00:03:16,000 --> 00:03:20,000
 x being the number of lifts to be produced per year

39
00:03:20,000 --> 00:03:26,000
 for our machine situation, so in-house situation

40
00:03:26,000 --> 00:03:34,000
 we have a variable cost and that variable cost is the cost of our operators

41
00:03:34,000 --> 00:03:37,000
 which we have there and of course we have the cost of our machines

42
00:03:37,000 --> 00:03:41,000
 which is given here for machine B, it's pretty simple

43
00:03:41,000 --> 00:03:47,000
 we only have a negative salvage value, we have an initial amount of money we need to invest

44
00:03:47,000 --> 00:03:50,000
 and we have our annual operating costs

45
00:03:50,000 --> 00:03:56,000
 for the first machine, machine A, we have our initial investment of 18,000

46
00:03:56,000 --> 00:04:00,000
 we have our salvage value of 2,000, positive 1 here

47
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:09,000
 we have an annual operating cost of 6,000 and we also have this overhaul that we need to do after 3 years

48
00:04:09,000 --> 00:04:21,000
 bringing those together, so bringing these together gives us the second part of our equation

49
00:04:21,000 --> 00:04:28,000
 and that equation can also be used to solve the second part of the question

50
00:04:28,000 --> 00:04:36,000
 okay, now of course we can have more than two alternatives, we can have three alternatives

51
00:04:36,000 --> 00:04:44,000
 a typical example here is conceptualized in the right part

52
00:04:44,000 --> 00:04:47,000
 it's a choice between the appropriate level of automation

53
00:04:47,000 --> 00:04:52,000
 what you see here is, this is a throughput, so basically it's the number of operations that you do

54
00:04:52,000 --> 00:05:00,000
 for instance in a mechanical workshop or in a warehouse

55
00:05:00,000 --> 00:05:07,000
 this is the cost, if you have a manual system, well starting costs are pretty low

56
00:05:07,000 --> 00:05:13,000
 but variable cost is pretty high because each time that our throughput is increasing

57
00:05:13,000 --> 00:05:19,000
 we will have to hire new people and that's rather expensive

58
00:05:19,000 --> 00:05:24,000
 the other extreme is to go for a fully automated system

59
00:05:24,000 --> 00:05:33,000
 which will require quite a steep investment but then a rather low variable cost

60
00:05:33,000 --> 00:05:37,000
 so then the question is what is the most interesting situation

61
00:05:37,000 --> 00:05:42,000
 and you see when we have a low throughput we favor the manual one

62
00:05:42,000 --> 00:05:46,000
 if you have a very high throughput we go for a fully automated one

63
00:05:46,000 --> 00:05:53,000
 this is a conceptual picture, this is a picture that is actually made by ABB

64
00:05:53,000 --> 00:06:05,000
 about robots, so what they are comparing is the systems, manual assembly, human robot collaboration

65
00:06:05,000 --> 00:06:10,000
 so called co-bots, robotic automation or fixed automation

66
00:06:10,000 --> 00:06:13,000
 and we have the production volume, we have the cost

67
00:06:13,000 --> 00:06:19,000
 and what you see is that with a very low production volume manual still seems to be the most interesting one

68
00:06:19,000 --> 00:06:25,000
 if you go for a very large production volume then probably fixed automation is the best

69
00:06:25,000 --> 00:06:30,000
 and in between we have a human robot co-bot zone and a robot zone

70
00:06:30,000 --> 00:06:37,000
 so the breakeven is providing insight in these things

71
00:06:37,000 --> 00:06:45,000
 we will not talk about Excel if you are interested you can go for Excel yourself

